# PART 4: ADVANCED PRODUCT VARIANT ENGINE

Build a real-world product variant management system.

Understand the difference between:

- Product Attributes: Characteristics or selectable properties.
- Product Variants: Actual purchasable combinations of those properties.

Example:

A T-shirt has:

Size: M, L, XL.

Color: Black, White.

The system can generate combinations:

- Black / M.
- Black / L.
- Black / XL.
- White / M.
- White / L.
- White / XL.

Each actual purchasable variant must support:

- Unique SKU.
- Barcode, if available.
- Variant-specific price.
- Compare-at/original price.
- Discount.
- Stock quantity.
- Low-stock threshold.
- Variant-specific image.
- Variant-specific weight.
- Variant-specific dimensions.
- Active/inactive status.
- Backorder permission, if enabled.

## Variant management requirements

- Automatically generate combinations when requested.
- Allow the admin to select which combinations actually exist.
- Allow manual variant creation.
- Allow individual variant editing.
- Allow bulk price updates.
- Allow bulk stock updates.
- Allow bulk SKU generation.
- Prevent duplicate SKUs.
- Prevent duplicate variant combinations within the same product.
- Support products with no variants.
- Support products with one or multiple selectable attributes.
- Support products where some attributes are informational only and do not generate variants.
- Support different prices and stock quantities for different variants.

IMPORTANT:

Do not automatically create every mathematically possible combination as a purchasable variant.

For example, if a product has three colors and five sizes, the admin must be able to disable unavailable combinations.

Only valid, active combinations should be purchasable.

